WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.378
This video is about MCP, the Model Context Protocol.

2
00:00:03.428 --> 00:00:07.742
The last video explained how one program gives one model one set of tools.

3
00:00:07.792 --> 00:00:11.482
MCP is how the whole industry agreed to share tools.

4
00:00:11.532 --> 00:00:21.768
You will add an MCP server to your agent in lab two, so the aim here is to understand what it is, what it is not, and what it costs you.

5
00:00:22.518 --> 00:00:31.748
MCP is an open standard for connecting an AI application to outside systems: files, databases, search, ticketing, anything with an interface.

6
00:00:31.798 --> 00:00:34.169
Anthropic released it in November 2024.

7
00:00:34.219 --> 00:00:42.643
In March 2025 OpenAI adopted it, Google followed, and by now the main agent frameworks and coding tools all speak it.

8
00:00:42.693 --> 00:00:47.485
The project's own analogy is a USB-C port: one connector, many devices.

9
00:00:47.535 --> 00:00:52.326
Before MCP, every tool integration was written for one application.

10
00:00:52.376 --> 00:00:59.186
If you had built a Jira tool for one agent framework, it did not work in another.

11
00:00:59.236 --> 00:01:03.221
MCP moves the integration to the tool's side.

12
00:01:03.271 --> 00:01:06.852
A Jira server written once works in any client.

13
00:01:07.602 --> 00:01:08.953
Three words you need.

14
00:01:09.003 --> 00:01:14.208
The host is your agent application, the thing that owns the model and the loop.

15
00:01:14.258 --> 00:01:17.360
Inside the host runs an MCP client.

16
00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:21.214
Each outside system is exposed by an MCP server.

17
00:01:21.264 --> 00:01:25.417
When the host starts, the client asks each server what it offers.

18
00:01:25.467 --> 00:01:36.276
Servers offer three kinds of things: tools, which the model can call; resources, which are data the host can read, such as files or records; and prompts, which are reusable instructions.

19
00:01:36.326 --> 00:01:46.435
The client turns the servers' tools into tool schemas, the model chooses one exactly as in the last video, and the client makes the call and returns the result.

20
00:01:46.485 --> 00:01:49.587
So from the model's point of view nothing changed.

21
00:01:49.637 --> 00:01:52.740
It still sees tool schemas and returns tool calls.

22
00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:56.944
What changed is where the tools come from and who maintains them.

23
00:01:57.693 --> 00:01:59.324
Servers come in two shapes.

24
00:01:59.374 --> 00:02:05.374
A local server runs on the same machine as the host and talks over standard input and output.

25
00:02:05.424 --> 00:02:09.407
That is how a coding agent on your laptop reaches your files.

26
00:02:09.457 --> 00:02:16.465
A remote server runs as a web service, talks over HTTP, and uses OAuth to sign users in.

27
00:02:16.515 --> 00:02:24.867
That is how a company exposes one system, say its ticket queue, to every agent in the building through one server with one access policy.

28
00:02:24.917 --> 00:02:33.941
For your project, a remote server is the shape to think about, because a service that depends on a file on someone's laptop is not a service.

29
00:02:34.691 --> 00:02:38.371
Now the limits, and they are the reason week five exists.

30
00:02:38.421 --> 00:02:41.083
MCP does not make tools safe.

31
00:02:41.133 --> 00:02:49.559
A server can expose a tool that deletes things behind a friendly name, and your client will pass it to the model like any other.

32
00:02:49.609 --> 00:02:51.932
MCP does not vet servers.

33
00:02:51.982 --> 00:02:57.018
Anyone can publish one, and a server you install is code running with your permissions.

34
00:02:57.068 --> 00:02:59.730
MCP does not shrink the context.

35
00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:09.562
Every server you connect adds its schemas to the window on every call, and twenty servers can mean hundreds of tool descriptions before the user has said a word.

36
00:03:09.612 --> 00:03:11.935
And MCP does not choose.

37
00:03:11.985 --> 00:03:17.360
The model still picks the tool, so a confusing server makes the agent worse, not better.

38
00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:29.904
In May 2025 researchers showed that a coding agent connected to GitHub's own MCP server could be steered, by a malicious issue in a public repository, into reading a private repository and publishing its contents.

39
00:03:29.954 --> 00:03:31.939
Nothing in the protocol was broken.

40
00:03:31.989 --> 00:03:36.346
The agent had the tools, and it followed instructions it found in data.

41
00:03:36.396 --> 00:03:37.363
Remember that case.

42
00:03:37.413 --> 00:03:40.076
We will take it apart in week five.

43
00:03:40.825 --> 00:03:46.152
So here is a checklist for lab two, when you add an MCP server to your agent.

44
00:03:46.202 --> 00:03:50.396
Who maintains it, and is it the system owner's official server or someone's weekend project?

45
00:03:50.446 --> 00:03:52.943
Which of its tools does your agent actually need?

46
00:03:52.993 --> 00:03:55.773
Most clients let you expose a subset, and you should.

47
00:03:55.823 --> 00:03:57.470
Does it read, write, or both?

48
00:03:57.520 --> 00:03:58.602
Writes get a gate.

49
00:03:58.652 --> 00:04:01.432
How many tokens do its schemas add to every call?

50
00:04:01.482 --> 00:04:02.847
Measure it in the trace.

51
00:04:02.897 --> 00:04:06.242
And can you run it where your data is allowed to be?

52
00:04:06.992 --> 00:04:07.944
To sum up.

53
00:04:07.994 --> 00:04:12.952
MCP is a standard for where tools come from, and it has won.

54
00:04:13.002 --> 00:04:16.291
Expect every system your organization buys to ship a server.

55
00:04:16.341 --> 00:04:23.302
It does not decide whether those tools are safe, how many you should expose, or what the model does with them.

56
00:04:23.352 --> 00:04:24.972
That is still your harness.

57
00:04:25.022 --> 00:04:31.315
Next video: the autonomy dial, which is how we talk about how much the harness lets the model decide.
